WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.480
I’m in Resolute, Nunavut, Canada,

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:04.339
the last stop on my Arctic voyage.

00:00:04.339 --> 00:00:08.150
It is one of the most northern and coldest
inhabited places in the world,

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:10.190
and the only reason I'm allowed to film here
right now

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:13.150
is because we have someone on polar bear watch.

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:14.860
There is some evidence of prehistoric settlement here,

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:16.980
but this town didn’t exist until 1947,

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:19.800
when the US and Canada built a weather station
here,

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.360
and it wasn’t populated until 1953,

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:26.640
when the government of Canada forcibly resettled
Inuit from Northern Quebec here

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.500
as part of a power play in the Cold War.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:32.439
The High Arctic relocation is a dark chapter
in Canada’s history,

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:35.640
and the stories of those who were moved into
appalling conditions,

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.890
are tales best told by people who were there,
not some British tourist.

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:41.399
There are links in the description for context,

00:00:41.399 --> 00:00:43.050
and I strongly recommend you watch them.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:47.269
But what I can talk about is why the 1950s
Canadian government

00:00:47.269 --> 00:00:49.850
thought it was worth uprooting people from
their homes

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:53.280
and moving them this far north.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.020
The North Pole, and most of the Arctic Ocean,
are international waters.

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:59.120
There’s no land below the ice,

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.819
so they’re treated the same as the Atlantic
or Pacific.

00:01:01.819 --> 00:01:03.000
But unlike countries further south,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.289
where it’s easy to say where your coastline
ends and the sea begins,

00:01:06.289 --> 00:01:08.811
up here in the north it’s a little bit more
complicated.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:12.409
In the fifties, there was an open legal question:

00:01:12.409 --> 00:01:14.600
if no one lived on these northern islands,

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:16.549
if they’re not even habitable,

00:01:16.549 --> 00:01:19.200
could they really be part of Canada?

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.810
Particularly when the United States is sending
hundreds of soldiers up here

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:25.340
to stake a claim, to shore up the defense
against Soviet Russia.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:29.560
The shortest route from Russia to America
for aircraft carrying nuclear bombs,

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:32.590
and later, for intercontinental ballistic
missiles,

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:34.070
was over the North Pole.

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:35.250
Over here.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.250
Over Resolute.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:39.620
And while the Canadian government were happy
to co-operate with America,

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:42.469
they were not about to risk being pushed out.

00:01:42.469 --> 00:01:44.899
The government's solution, back then:

00:01:44.899 --> 00:01:47.109
move some people who they think don’t matter,

00:01:47.109 --> 00:01:48.890
and make it clear to the world:

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:50.340
this is Canadian territory.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:51.340
Look.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:52.460
Canadians live here.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:54.950
The repercussions from that still go on today,

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:57.079
and so does the argument about sovereignty
up here:

00:01:57.079 --> 00:01:58.880
just for different reasons.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:00.969
Because while the Cold War has ended,

00:02:00.969 --> 00:02:05.430
and missiles will hopefully not be flying
over the North Pole any time soon,

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:08.630
there is now a very different question.

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:10.420
Climate change means that the Northwest Passage,

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:13.560
the once legendary sea route through the north
of Canada,

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:17.360
is now theoretically open for shipping a few
months a year.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:21.910
The north of Canada is not just frozen ice
that goes all the way to the pole,

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:25.000
and most of the maps you see are really misleading.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.450
This here is one of a cluster of islands,

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:29.690
nowhere near as big as they look on most maps,

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:33.650
and if there’s no ice between them, it’s
a shortcut.

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:38.099
The Arctic is now a possible route between
Europe and the west coast of America,

00:02:38.099 --> 00:02:39.610
instead of going through the Panama Canal:

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:43.600
and that cuts over 4000 miles off the journey.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:45.620
And the first cruise ship has already gone
through:

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:47.510
by the time you watch this, it will have gone
through.

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:50.810
And it's not an old icebreaker with a hundred
people on it like I’m travelling on,

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:54.740
but a massive cruise ship with thousands of
people,

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.490
eight restaurants, and a casino.

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:00.830
Like it or not, this town, this space here:

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:02.980
this is part of a tourist route now.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:06.870
Now, Canada says some of the Northwest Passage
is part of their territorial waters,

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:09.290
almost like a big, wide river.

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:10.870
Anyone going through should be cleared with
them,

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:12.710
be subject to Canadian law, and perhaps in
future,

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:14.370
they should pay a tax.

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:15.400
The US and Europe?

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.070
They say it’s international waters and they
can do whatever they want.

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:20.310
And the consequences could be serious:

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.370
if this does become a major shipping route,

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:25.180
then sooner or later there’s going to be
a major shipping accident,

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:29.820
particularly if climate change brings unpredictable
storms to the ocean here.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:33.690
The idea of more oil being spilled in the
Arctic is worrying a lot of people.

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:37.510
In a few decades, as the ice melts, more channels
open,

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:40.030
and drilling for oil and gas becomes possible,

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:42.320
our next international battles,

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:45.790
political or otherwise, might be for the Arctic:

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:49.160
not just about who owns it, but for its entire
future.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:51.980
Hi, I'm Chris Hadfield, this is...

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:52.960
I'm Tom Scott!

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:54.880
This is Tom Scott, we're here in the Arctic.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.600
That's it from Generator Arctic!

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:57.100
That's all!

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:57.600
Thank you very much!

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.320
It's been an incredible experience, thanks
for coming along,

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:03.160
and go the web site that's written, you can
see all the details.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.560
And go see their show, Massey Hall, November
12th...

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:07.100
November 12th in Toronto!

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:09.740
Chris, there's the camera,
take it, go for it!

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:12.780
Take it, submerge it, go!

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:22.420
Hey, it's not so bad!

